{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"L01\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The definite noun ٱلْحَمْدُ presents praise as the clause’s topic before assigning it to Allah, without naming a human praiser or a finite act of praising. The supplied range of حَمْد keeps commendation of worth and gratitude for benefit available together.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"}],\"activation\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ opens 1:2 as a noun; later, نَعْبُدُ explicitly names the worshippers’ action.\",\"limits\":\"The clause does not make every shade of حَمْد equally active, or identify a particular human act of praise.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"L02\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The lām in لِلَّهِ assigns ٱلْحَمْدُ to Allah as belonging to Him or due to Him. The same name shifts from its place in the opening بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ phrase to the recipient of an explicit praise attribution; the bound form لِلَّهِ keeps the relation attached to the name in recitation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The change from بِـ in 1:1 to لِ in 1:2 accompanies the shift from the opening invocation to the praise clause.\",\"limits\":\"The lām marks assignment or due-ness; it does not by itself encode exclusivity or settle whether exclusivity comes from the clause’s full context.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"L03\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"wrong\"}\n{\"id\":\"L04\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"wrong\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Could ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ carry an association with the ع ل م senses of knowing and distinguishing signs, so that the worlds are heard as knowable, sign-bearing orders? If active, this would make the final plural scope legible as well as cosmic.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The verse names the worlds but does not explicitly connect them to knowledge or signs. A lexicon entry establishing that link for this noun, or Quranic usage pairing ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ with knowledge or signs, could show whether the association is active here.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
